Topic 3. 3.1 #3, Other Infrastructure Concepts
Where things live and who’s in charge:
On-premises. Everything is in your own building. You have full control, but you also have full responsibility: physical security, power, patching, the lot. It’s owning
yoar car outright. Centralised vs decentralised. Centralised means one head office controls everything. It’s easy to manage and monitor, but 
it’s a single point of failure. Decentralised spreads control across many locations, so it’s more resilient but harder to keep consistent. Think of one big hospital vs
a network of local clinics.

Ways to run software:
Virtualisation. One physical computer runs several full virtual machines, each with its own complete operating system. Picture separate houses on one block of land. 
Wrote up VM escape from 2.3. Containers. Lighter than VMs. Containers share the host’s operating system but keep each app in its own box. Picture flats in 
one apartment building: they share the foundations and plumbing, so if the shared base has a flaw, every flat is exposed.

Special devices that are hard to secure:
IoT. Smart devices like cameras, smart TVs and fridges. They often ship with weak default passwords and rarely get updates. That’s why they belong on a separate network,
like a guest Wi-Fi plan. SCADA / ICS. The systems that run power stations, water treatment and factories. Breaches here can cause physical harm, which is why they’re 
the classic air-gap case. RTOS (Real-Time Operating System). Systems that must respond instantly, such as car braking systems or medical equipment. 
You can’t pause them for a security scan or a reboot, so security must be built in from the start. Embedded systems. Computers built into a device to do one job,
like a pacemaker or a smart meter. They’re often impossible to patch, so the security has to be right before they leave the factory.

High availability:
Keeping systems running even when something fails, using backup hardware that takes over automatically. Think of a hospital’s backup generator. The trade-off is cost,
because you’re paying for spares you hope never to use.





